## Why This Is Asked
This question explores a privacy-preserving on-device machine learning use case with real-world constraints including energy efficiency, latency requirements, data minimization principles, and regulatory compliance. It emphasizes the importance of measurable go/no-go criteria and a concrete break-even timeline for implementation decisions.

## Key Concepts
- On-device machine learning models (autoencoder/isolation forest)
- Energy and performance budgeting for background tasks
- Data minimization and privacy impact assessments
- Go/No-Go criteria and break-even horizon analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function detectAnomaly(readings, threshold){
  const mean = readings.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/readings.length;
  const variance = readings.reduce((a,b)=>a+Math.pow(b-mean,2),0)/readings.length;
  return Math.abs(readings[readings.length-1] - mean) > threshold * Math.sqrt(variance);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to translate connectivity challenges into a minimal, privacy-conscious on-device flow with cost trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Edge batching, offline queues, deduping
- Energy/memory budgeting, latency impact
- Privacy, consent, rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function batchRequests(requests, maxBatch) {
  const batches = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < requests.length; i += maxBatch) {
    batches.push(requests.slice(i, i + maxBatch));
  }
  return batches;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle failed batches in poor connectivity differently for iOS vs Android?
- What telemetry would you collect to validate break-even without compromising privacy?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about cost-benefit tradeoffs for on-device prefetching and privacy-friendly design in a beginner-friendly context.

## Key Concepts
- On-device caching and prefetching strategies
- Resource budgets: energy, memory, data
- Privacy and consent controls
- Observability: cache hit rate, latency, drift
- Rollout strategy and break-even planning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight prefetch example (pseudo)
async function prefetchAssets(urls, cache) {
  for (const u of urls) {
    try {
      const resp = await fetch(u, {mode: 'no-cors', credentials: 'omit'});
      if (resp.ok) await cache.put(u, resp.clone());
    } catch (e) { /* ignore */ }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure energy impact on mobile vs desktop?
- How would you ensure privacy compliance and opt-in UX for prefetching?

## Why This Is Asked
CBA for an offline-first backup feature requires tangible cost knobs and measurable outcomes beyond accuracy or latency; beginner-friendly but demonstrates ability to quantify trade-offs and plan rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Energy budgeting per photo and dedup impact
- Data minimization and privacy/compliance
- Rollout, offline fallback, drift/maintenance, break-even

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo energy check before upload
function shouldUpload(photoSizeMB, currentEnergyJ, thresholdJ){
  const dedupFactor = 1.5;
  const energyPerMB = 0.25;
  const energy = photoSizeMB * energyPerMB * dedupFactor;
  return (currentEnergyJ - energy) > thresholdJ;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure de-dup effectiveness in production?
- What metrics trigger rollback during rollout?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model privacy-preserving analytics, not ML, with real-world trade-offs across storage, compute, energy, and compliance.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy budgets & CRDTs for local aggregation
- Edge-resource constraints and telemetry design
- Compliance costs and regulatory penalties

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple CRDT-like Grow-Only Counter
class GCounter { constructor() { this.counters = new Map(); }
inc(k, v=1){ this.counters.set(k, Math.max((this.counters.get(k)||0)+v,0)); }
merge(other){ for(const [k,v] of other.counters){ this.counters.set(k, Math.max((this.counters.get(k)||0), v)); } }
value(){ let s=0; for(const v of this.counters.values()) s+=v; return s; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle CRDT reconciliation when offline and merging after sync?
- What metrics would you track to detect drift in your aggregates and how would you price the analytics feature?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical cost-benefit analysis thinking for privacy-conscious mobile features with offline-first architecture. It tests understanding of storage trade-offs, energy budgeting, consent management, and long-term maintenance considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first storage and local geofence processing to ensure reliability without network connectivity
- Energy budgeting for continuous location monitoring and CPU usage for geofence checks
- Privacy, consent, and data minimization requirements for location-based features
- Cloud synchronization trade-offs for shared calendar data and conflict resolution
- Rollout planning with phased deployment and offline fallback behavior
- Technical debt and drift/maintenance costs over the feature lifecycle

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: register geofence, trigger locally, and schedule reminder
registerGeofence(reminder.id, reminder.coordinates, reminder.radius, async (trigger) => {
  const localReminder = await db.getReminder(reminder.id);
  if (localReminder) {
    await notificationManager.show(localReminder);
    await syncQueue.add({ reminderId: reminder.id, triggeredAt: Date.now() });
  }
});
```
